multiplayer game
"flat earth" theme
players start on an island, could be U.S. or some island nearby
The earth is not really "flat", that's just stupid.  It's much much bigger than we're taught.
The map we're taught within the overall "flat earth map" is considered "inside".
Inside, things are relatively safe, minimal combat if any.

inner world:
-

Ideas:

That combination can work very well for a long-term multiplayer sandbox because the systems reinforce each other instead of existing as isolated professions. The key is designing the world topology first so every tech tree has geographic pressure and economic dependence.

1. World Structure — “Flat Earth Rings”

Your map topology is the foundation for progression, politics, logistics, and exploration.

A strong version of your concept:

Core Layout
                [Outer Void / Ice Wall]
        -------------------------------------
        |                                   |
        |        OUTER RING CONTINENT       |
        |   (high danger / late-game tech)  |
        |                                   |
        |-----------------------------------|
        |                                   |
        |         GREAT OCEAN RING          |
        |                                   |
        |-----------------------------------|
        |                                   |
        |        INNER RING CONTINENT       |
        |   (mid-game kingdoms & industry)  |
        |                                   |
        |-----------------------------------|
        |                                   |
        |         INNER SEA / CRADLE        |
        |                                   |
        |-----------------------------------|
        |            CENTRAL LAND           |
        -------------------------------------

This structure naturally creates:

increasing danger outward
logistical bottlenecks
trade routes
naval importance
migration pressure
meaningful exploration

You can scale this indefinitely because rings are easier to procedurally expand than square maps.

2. Tile Architecture (Critical for Scale)

Do NOT think of the world as “a giant bitmap.”

Think:

Hierarchical Streaming World
World
 ├── Ring
 │    ├── Region
 │    │    ├── Chunk
 │    │    │    ├── Tile

Recommended:

Level	Size
Tile	1m–4m
Chunk	64x64 tiles
Region	64x64 chunks
Ring Segment	many regions

This lets:

clients stream nearby chunks
servers shard regions
procedural generation remain deterministic
persistence scale horizontally

For multiplayer, deterministic generation is extremely important.

Use:

world_seed + ring_id + region_id + chunk_id

to regenerate terrain without storing everything.

Store only:

player modifications
structures
ownership
harvested resources
roads
cities
3. Ring Biome Philosophy

Each ring should support different technological eras.

Central Land

Safe beginner civilization.

Resources:

copper
tin
herbs
common woods
livestock

Gameplay:

farming
commerce
starter guilds
basic magic
Inner Ring

Industrial expansion.

Resources:

iron
silver
magical forests
dangerous fauna
deeper oceans

Gameplay:

metallurgy
kingdoms
shipping
faction wars
enchantment
Outer Ring

Harsh survival and high-tier progression.

Resources:

mythic metals
dimensional flora
spaceflight materials
ancient ruins

Gameplay:

advanced magic
relic tech
space travel
cosmic threats
4. Magic System Design

The biggest mistake MMORPGs make:
magic becomes “press fireball.”

Instead:
magic should be an ecosystem.

Better Model

Magic comes from:

biological harvesting
celestial timing
geography
rituals
crafted catalysts

Example:

Source	Example
Flowers	mana pigments
Trees	living resin
Animals	essence glands
Moon phase	ritual multiplier
Volcano regions	fire affinity
Tiered Magic

Avoid simple:

Magic Level 1 -> 2 -> 3

Instead use:

Discovery
Refinement
Combination
Specialization
Ascension

Example:

Fire Path
Ash Flower
 + Ember Resin
 + Salamander Gland
 = Ignition Ink

Then:

Ignition Ink + Silver Catalyst
= Rune of Combustion

This creates:

exploration economy
trade
experimentation
hidden recipes

Players LOVE discovery systems.

5. Metals & Alloy System

This should become the backbone of warfare and infrastructure.

Core Metals
Tier	Examples
Basic	copper, tin
Industrial	iron, steel
Noble	silver, gold
Advanced	mythril, void iron
Cosmic	starsteel
Alloying

Do NOT make alloys linear upgrades only.

Make them trait-based.

Example:

Alloy	Trait
Steel	durability
Silversteel	anti-magic
Void Iron	corruption resistance
Sun Alloy	heat conduction

Then enchantments interact with alloy traits.

This creates:

meaningful crafting
regional specialization
real markets
6. Enchantment System

Enchantments should depend on:

metal composition
rune quality
magical reagents
celestial conditions

Example:

Weapon Quality =
smith skill
+ alloy purity
+ enchantment compatibility
+ ritual timing

This allows true “master craftsmen.”

That creates:

player reputation
artisan economies
guild monopolies
7. Space Travel Tech Tree

This is the correct idea:
space travel should NOT be a separate game.

It should emerge from all systems.

Dependencies

Space tech requires:

advanced metallurgy
magical energy
navigation sciences
large-scale industry
guild financing

This naturally forces civilization development.

Suggested Progression
Astronomy
→ Navigation
→ Energy Manipulation
→ Advanced Alloys
→ Atmospheric Vehicles
→ Orbital Flight
→ Planetary Gates
Why This Works

Only major civilizations can realistically achieve it.

That creates:

mega-projects
politics
economic wars
espionage
guild alliances
8. Business Guild System (Very Smart Addition)

This dramatically broadens your player base.

Many sandbox games fail because:
“everyone must fight.”

Instead, allow:

merchants
bankers
caravan operators
ship builders
insurers
land owners
industrialists

These players stabilize your economy.

9. Banking System

Be VERY careful here.

Player economies collapse if:

infinite currency enters system
no item sinks exist
banks duplicate value
Better Banking Design

Banks should:

store goods securely
issue loans
fund expeditions
insure cargo
finance cities

But:

loans can default
banks can fail
trade routes matter

This creates emergent gameplay.

10. PvP Risk Geography

This is where your ring world becomes powerful.

Safer Inner Areas
lower resource quality
protected trade
lower rewards
Outer Areas
legendary resources
high PvP
dangerous creatures
rare ruins

This naturally creates:

risk vs reward

without hard forcing PvP.

11. The Most Important Technical Decision

You are not building:

“a game with systems”

You are building:

“an economy simulator disguised as a fantasy world”

The strongest MMOs are actually:

logistics simulators
social systems
resource economies
political ecosystems

The combat is only one layer.

12. Recommended Server Architecture

For this scale:

Recommended Stack
Server
Rust or Go for simulation
ECS architecture
authoritative server
Networking
UDP + reliability layer
snapshot interpolation
World Storage
PostgreSQL
Redis cache
chunk persistence
Procedural Generation
layered noise
deterministic seeds
biome masks
climate simulation
13. Procedural Generation Strategy

Use:

radial distance from center
latitude bands
moisture simulation
tectonic masks

Because your world is ring-based, distance from center becomes a major gameplay variable.

Example:

center_distance -> influences:
- temperature
- corruption
- mana density
- creature tiers

This is MUCH more interesting than standard rectangular maps.

14. Long-Term Retention

Your game will survive only if:
players create stories.

Systems should support:

trade wars
guild alliances
expeditions
discoveries
monopolies
city building
religion
piracy
exploration

The best sandbox MMOs become:

“history generators.”

15. One Extremely Important Warning

Do NOT start by building:

space travel
banking
advanced magic
politics

Start with:

terrain generation
chunk streaming
movement/networking
persistence
harvesting
crafting
economy

If the foundation is weak, all higher systems fail.

16. Recommended MVP

Your first playable version should only include:

MVP Systems
one ring
chunked world
harvesting
crafting
metallurgy
simple combat
player trading
basic towns

No space travel yet.

No advanced banking yet.

Prove:

server scaling
economy stability
fun exploration loop

before expanding.

17. Strong Design Direction

Your concept is strongest if you lean into:

Civilization progression
+
geographic scarcity
+
economic interdependence
+
exploration
+
player-created history

instead of:

theme-park quest MMO

You’re much closer to:

survival sandbox
civilization simulator
economy MMO
exploration MMO

which is a rarer and potentially much more compelling direction.

